{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"silk-unpleasant-jackal","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe S3 dry run (`democratic-untiring-saiga`) must measure the reclaim on a shadow clone of the real store. The S1 engine cannot finish at that size. `collapse_shadow_tracker` (`src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_collapse.py:63`) calls `_fast_import_tree_commit` (`reclaim_collapse.py:375`) once per commit: a new `git fast-import` process, `deleteall`, a full `ls-tree -r` of the commit (`_tree_entries`, `:363`), and one `git cat-file blob` subprocess per blob (`_blob_bytes_for_oid`, `:425`) to look for SNAPSHOTs. Measured read-only on 2026-11-16: `origin/tickets` has 309,527 commits, 13,119 of them in the last 30 days, and 220,989 files in the tip tree. Replaying the commits above a 30-day horizon therefore costs about 13,119 × 220,989 ≈ 2.9 billion `cat-file` processes. The run would never finish, so the epic's predicted shrink cannot be measured.\n\n## Approach\n\nKeep the engine's contract and its tests: same `CollapseResult`, same refs, the same `old_to_new` map and the same ledger rewrites. Change only how the commits are written:\n\n1. **One `git fast-import` process** for the whole rewrite. Each new commit gets a mark (`:1`, `:2`, ...). Its parents come from `from`/`merge` mark references. `--export-marks` writes the marks file, and reading it back gives each new SHA for `old_to_new` and `CollapseResult`.\n2. **Checkpoint by tree reference.** The parentless checkpoint uses `M 040000 <boundary-tree> \"\"`, which sets the root tree in one command (probed with git 2.x: the imported commit's tree equals the source tree id). Inline `M` entries then override only the SNAPSHOT blobs whose ledger positions need re-resolution.\n3. **Replayed commits apply only their own changes.** For each commit in `boundary..tip`, `git diff-tree -r -z --raw --no-renames <first-parent> <commit>` lists the changed paths. Those become `M` (mode, blob id) or `D` lines on top of the rewritten first parent. Merge commits add `merge` lines for their other parents. Unchanged SNAPSHOTs are inherited from the already-rewritten parent tree, so only changed SNAPSHOT paths need rewriting.\n4. **Read only SNAPSHOT blobs**, those whose path ends in `-SNAPSHOT.json`, through one long-lived `git cat-file --batch` process instead of a process per blob.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_collapse.py`: stream construction, the diff-based replay, the batched blob reader, and marks read-back. Move the stream helpers into a new `src/rebar/_commands/reclaim_collapse_stream.py` if the file would pass the 800-line cap.\n- `tests/unit/test_reclaim_collapse.py`: keep every existing test, and add the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTDD, red first, on throwaway shadow repositories under `tmp_path`.\n\n- Equivalence: for a fixture with a merge commit, a deleted file, and SNAPSHOTs both above and below the boundary, the rewritten trees, metadata and ledger rewrites match those of the current per-commit engine. The test computes the expected trees from the existing algorithm in the test before the swap.\n- Cost bound: on a fixture with 200 files and 50 replayed commits, the engine runs `fast-import` once and `cat-file` only for SNAPSHOT blobs. A counting wrapper around the engine's git seam checks this.\n- Failure path: a fast-import error still raises `ReclaimCollapseError` and leaves the shadow branch unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] All existing tests in `tests/unit/test_reclaim_collapse.py` pass unchanged.\n- [ ] The new equivalence test shows identical rewritten trees, commit metadata and ledger rewrites for a fixture with merges, deletions and SNAPSHOTs on both sides of the boundary.\n- [ ] The new cost test shows one `fast-import` process per collapse and `cat-file` reads only for SNAPSHOT paths.\n- [ ] `make verify` is run and any failure is fixed or shown environmental; the Gerrit change gets `Verified +1`; `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exit 0.\n","id":"11aa-b3fb-f239-4f7a","parent_id":"536b-8930-b922-4063","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S1 collapse engine: one fast-import stream over changed paths, so a real-size store can be rewritten"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1794874461213203870,"uuid":"2bb9ecbc-e82d-4157-b0a6-7a0ca616a242"}